.TH WHOSENT 1 "2026-10-11" "whosent manual" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
whosent \- see who sent an agent: a viewer for JWT token exchange, Biscuit, UCAN, Mandatum and macaroon delegation chains
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whosent
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR | \fB\-\fR]
.br
.B whosent check
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-strict\fR] [\fIFILE\fR | \fB\-\fR]
.br
.B whosent ask
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIREQUEST OPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR | \fB\-\fR]
.br
.B whosent demo
[\fB\-\-fixed\fR] [\fB\-\-list\fR] [\fB\-\-emit\fR \fIDIR\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR]] [\fISCENARIO\fR]
.br
.B whosent completion
\fISHELL\fR
.br
.B whosent version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B whosent
opens a delegation chain in a terminal UI and shows who it is rooted in, how
authority narrows at every hop, and where trust breaks. It reads three
formats:
.TP
.B jwt
OAuth 2.0 token exchange (RFC 8693): a JWT whose nested \fBact\fR claims name
who acted for its subject, including ID-JAG (\fBtyp: oauth-id-jag+jwt\fR),
Txn-Tokens (\fBtyp: txntoken+jwt\fR) and Microsoft Entra agent tokens. One
token's prior actors are the issuer's word (RFC 8693 section 4.1) and are drawn
as
.IR asserted ,
never as verified. Several tokens from successive exchanges, oldest first, are
checked hop by hop; those hop rules (J7 to J10) are whosent's own consistency
checks, not a procedure any specification defines.
.TP
.B biscuit
A Biscuit token: an authority block signed by a root key, then attenuation
blocks that can only add checks. Signatures, third-party blocks, open and
sealed proofs, and the token's Datalog checks are evaluated by whosent's own
code, which agrees with the reference on all 38 tokens of the specification's
sample corpus.
.TP
.B ucan
A UCAN 1.0 chain: delegations, each signed by its issuer's did:key and
addressed to the next, ending in an invocation whose prf lists them from the
subject's own. Everything is checked by whosent's own code, which agrees with
the specification's 1.0.0 fixtures.
.TP
.B mandatum
A Mandatum chain of signed links from a named human down to the acting agent.
On every run whosent also hands the chain and the same answers to the Mandatum
v0.2.0 reference verifier it embeds; if the two disagree, the reference verdict
wins and the exit status is 70.
.TP
.B macaroon
A macaroon and its discharges, in the libmacaroons formats or as a Fly.io
token. One HMAC chain covers every caveat: without \fB\-\-root\-key\fR none is
vouched for. whosent checks it with its own code and compares the verdict with
go-macaroon or superfly/macaroon on every run; a disagreement exits 70.
.PP
Every rule of the format is evaluated for every link. Unlike a verifier, which
stops at the first failure, whosent reports all of them, and draws everything
below the first broken link as unreached: no authority reaches it.
.PP
A rule that needs an input you did not give is reported as
.IR "not checked" ,
never as passing: signatures need keys (\fB\-\-jwks\fR, \fB\-\-trust\fR or
\fB\-\-root\-key\fR), the audience rule needs \fB\-\-audience\fR, Biscuit checks
need an authorizer (\fB\-\-fact\fR, \fB\-\-authorizer\fR), macaroon predicates
need \fB\-\-fact\fR, and revocation needs a
list or a set. A chain is only reported
.B VALID
when every rule was checked for every link and held.
.SH INPUT
The chain is read from \fIFILE\fR, or from standard input when \fIFILE\fR is
\fB\-\fR or standard input is a pipe; the interactive view then reads keys from
the terminal. The format is recognised from the input; \fB\-\-format\fR names it
when the input could be more than one, and an input two formats could claim is
refused rather than guessed at.
.TP
.B JWT
One compact JWS (a leading \fBBearer\fR prefix is dropped), or several, as a
JSON array of strings or one per line, oldest first.
.TP
.B Biscuit
URL-safe base64, padded or not, with or without a \fBbiscuit:\fR prefix, or the
raw protobuf bytes.
.TP
.B UCAN
A container (ucan-wg/container v0.1.0, any of its six headers), a JSON array
of base64 tokens, or base64 tokens one per line.
.TP
.B Mandatum
A JSON array of compact JWS strings, sponsor first; a JSON-RPC
\fBtools/call\fR request whose \fBparams._meta\fR carries the chain under
\fBio.github.kanywst.mandatum/chain\fR, read exactly as Mandatum v0.2.0's MCP
middleware reads it; or one serialization per line, with blank lines and lines
starting with \fB#\fR skipped.
.TP
.B Macaroon
libmacaroons binary v1 or v2, raw or base64 in any alphabet, a root and its
discharges back to back or separated by whitespace or commas; their JSON, one
object or an array; or a Fly.io token (\fBFlyV1 fm2_...\fR, comma-joined).
.PP
Bytes inside a serialization are never altered. Input is limited to 1 MiB; a
Mandatum chain to 64 links, a JWT bundle to 64 tokens and 64 nested actors, and
a Biscuit token to 32 blocks of 256 KiB each; a UCAN chain to 64 tokens; and a
macaroon input to 16 macaroons of 128 caveats.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B check
Evaluate every rule for every link and print a report: the verdict, a rule grid
per link, every finding, and how authority narrows at each hop. With
\fB\-\-json\fR the report is printed as JSON (schema
.BR whosent.chain/v1 ,
the same for every format) and nothing else reaches standard output; with
\fB\-\-native\fR as well, the format's own report is printed instead
(Mandatum's is
.BR whosent.report/v1 ).
.TP
.B ask
Check a request against the chain with its format's own matcher, at every link,
so you can see where authority for it ends, or where it appears from nowhere.
Each format takes its own question: a JWT a scope (\fB\-\-scope\fR, used at
\fB\-\-audience\fR); a Biscuit token a request its authorizer (\fB\-\-authorizer\fR,
\fB\-\-fact\fR) runs over it, with \fB\-\-resource\-id\fR and \fB\-\-action\fR
becoming resource(...) and operation(...) facts and \fB\-\-policy\fR replacing the
authorizer's policies (which makes the answer at most CONDITIONAL); a UCAN chain
a command (\fB\-\-action\fR) and its arguments (\fB\-\-field\fR
args=\fIJSON\fR); Mandatum a
resource request (\fB\-\-resource\-id\fR, \fB\-\-action\fR,
\fB\-\-resource\-type\fR, \fB\-\-tag\fR, \fB\-\-attr\fR).
\fB\-\-field\fR \fIKEY\fR=\fIVALUE\fR sets any question field by name. A flag
the format has no use for is refused, and nothing a question needs is filled in
from a default.
The answer is labelled
.B AUTHORITATIVE
(the chain verified with every input supplied),
.B CONDITIONAL
(nothing failed, but some rules were not checked) or
.B HYPOTHETICAL
(the chain does not verify, so it permits nothing; this is what the leaf
claims). With \fB\-\-json\fR the answer is printed as schema
.BR whosent.ask/v1 .
.TP
.B demo
Mint example chains with throwaway keys and open them in the interactive view.
With no \fISCENARIO\fR it tours them all, every format in turn. Each scenario
brings its own keys and inputs, so the input options do not apply. With
\fB\-\-emit\fR \fIDIR\fR it writes one scenario's files instead and prints the
\fBwhosent check\fR command that checks them, with a
.I manifest.json
naming the scenario and holding that command (a Mandatum scenario's also
records what it must report). Files are written 0600 through a
temporary file and a rename, so a planted symbolic link is replaced rather than
written through; a directory that is not empty is refused without
\fB\-\-force\fR. \fB\-\-list\fR prints every scenario with its format.
.TP
.B completion
Print a completion script for \fBbash\fR, \fBzsh\fR, \fBfish\fR or
\fBpowershell\fR.
.TP
.B version
Print whosent's version and the version of the Mandatum reference verifier
it embeds.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-format " NAME"
The chain's format, when the input could be more than one: \fBjwt\fR,
\fBbiscuit\fR, \fBucan\fR or \fBmandatum\fR.
By default the format is recognised from the input, and an input two formats
could claim is refused rather than guessed at.
.TP
.BI \-\-trust " FILE"
A trust file mapping issuers to their key documents:
.B {"issuers": {"ISSUER": {"jwks_file": "PATH"} | {"jwks_url": "https://..."}}}.
A relative \fBjwks_file\fR is resolved against the trust file's directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-jwks " ISSUER=PATH|ISSUER=https://URL"
One issuer's keys, for JWT and Mandatum chains. Repeatable. For a JWT, RSA, EC
and Ed25519 keys are read (RS, PS, ES and EdDSA); encryption keys, symmetric
keys and duplicate kids are skipped or refused. For a Mandatum chain, keys are
read the way Mandatum's JWKS resolver reads them: Ed25519 only, and a document
it would refuse is refused here.
URLs must be https, including after any redirect; a fetch is capped at 1 MiB
and 5 seconds. Issuers come only from these options, never from the chain, so
a chain cannot make whosent fetch anything. A JWT's \fBjku\fR, \fBx5u\fR,
\fBx5c\fR and \fBjwk\fR headers are not used.
.TP
.BI \-\-root\-key " KEY"
A public key a Biscuit token may be rooted in, as hex
(\fBed25519/\fR\fIHEX\fR or \fBsecp256r1/\fR\fIHEX\fR) or a file holding it.
Repeatable. Without it, the authority block's signature is not checked; the
later blocks are still verified against each other. For a macaroon, the root
key itself (a Fly.io signing key), as hex, \fBtext:\fR\fISECRET\fR, or a file.
.TP
.BI \-\-revocations " FILE"
A published compact revocation set, for Mandatum chains (specification
section 7.1).
.TP
.BI \-\-revoked\-jtis " FILE"
A list of revoked identifiers, one per line. For a Biscuit token, block
revocation ids: a block on the list is revoked. For a UCAN chain, delegation
CIDs, in base58btc (z...) or base32 (b...). For a Mandatum chain, the exact
jti list used to resolve a hit in the revocation set's filter; without it, a
hit denies, as the specification says.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-age " DURATION"
The oldest revocation set accepted; \fB0\fR accepts any age. Default 24h. A
stale set makes revocation unknown, which denies.
.TP
.BI \-\-audience " AUDIENCE"
The party the chain is presented to; checks rule J4 (JWT) or V9 (Mandatum).
An empty value is an error.
.TP
.BI \-\-fact " FACT"
A Datalog fact the authorizer supplies, such as \fBoperation("read")\fR, for
evaluating a Biscuit token's checks. For a macaroon, a predicate the verifier
satisfies exactly, such as \fBaccount = 3735928559\fR. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-authorizer " FILE"
A file of Biscuit authorizer Datalog (facts, checks and policies) to evaluate
the token's checks against. Without it or \fB\-\-fact\fR, only expiry checks
are evaluated, against the evaluation time.
.TP
.BI \-\-at " TIME"
Evaluate at this time instead of now: RFC 3339, unix seconds, \fBnow\fR, or a
relative offset such as \fB+25m\fR or \fB\-1h\fR. A Mandatum revocation set's
age is measured against this time too, and a Biscuit token's checks see it as
\fBtime(...)\fR unless the authorizer supplies its own.
.TP
.BI \-\-skew " DURATION"
Clock skew tolerance, at most 5m. Default 30s.
.TP
.B \-\-reverse
A Mandatum chain was handed over leaf first; reverse it before checking. Without this,
a chain that looks reversed is reported, never silently reordered.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-motion
No animation in the interactive view.
.TP
.B \-\-ascii
ASCII glyphs only. Also set by \fBWHOSENT_ASCII=1\fR.
.SH CHECK OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the report as JSON (schema \fBwhosent.chain/v1\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-native
With \fB\-\-json\fR, print the format's own report instead (Mandatum's
schema is \fBwhosent.report/v1\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Treat INCOMPLETE as a failure (exit 1).
.SH ASK OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-resource\-type " TYPE"
The resource's type. Default \fBmcp_tool\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-resource\-id " ID"
The resource, such as a tool name. Defaults to the tool a tools/call request
names.
.TP
.BI \-\-action " NAME"
The action attempted. Default \fBinvoke\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-tag " TAG"
A tag the deployment attaches to the resource. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-attr " KEY=VALUE"
An attribute conditions are evaluated against. \fIVALUE\fR is read as JSON when
it parses, so \fBargs.n=3\fR is a number. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-attr\-from\-args
Map a tools/call request's arguments to \fBargs.\fR\fIname\fR attributes. This
mapping is whosent's assumption, not something an enforcement point does, and
a warning says so.
.TP
.BI \-\-request " FILE"
Read the request from a JSON file instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-scope " SCOPE"
JWT: the scope to ask about. Required for a JWT question.
.TP
.BI \-\-policy " POLICIES"
Biscuit: the allow and deny policies that decide, instead of the authorizer's
own. The answer is then at most CONDITIONAL.
.TP
.BI \-\-field " KEY=VALUE"
Set one of the format's question fields by name, such as
\fBfacts=resource("orders"); operation("read")\fR for Biscuit. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the answer as JSON, in the format's own schema (\fBwhosent.ask/v1\fR for
Mandatum).
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Treat a CONDITIONAL answer as not permitted.
.SH RULES
.SS JWT
.TP
.B J1
Token: a compact JWS with a JSON header and claims, signed with RS, PS, ES or
EdDSA. \fBalg: none\fR, HMAC and unknown critical headers are refused.
.TP
.B J2
Signature: verifies against the keys configured for the issuer the token names.
.TP
.B J3
Time: \fBiat\fR, \fBnbf\fR and \fBexp\fR within the skew. The last token must
hold now; an earlier one, when the next was issued.
.TP
.B J4
Audience: the last token is addressed to \fB\-\-audience\fR.
.TP
.B J5
Actor: the current actor is an object with a \fBsub\fR. A missing \fBiss\fR is
a warning, a missing \fBsub_profile\fR a note.
.TP
.B J6
may_act: an exchanged token's actor is the party the previous token's
\fBmay_act\fR names.
.TP
.B J7
Extends: an exchanged token's \fBact\fR chain is the previous one plus exactly
one new actor.
.TP
.B J8
Scope: an exchanged token's scope is a subset of the previous token's.
.TP
.B J9
Subject: the subject does not change across an exchange.
.TP
.B J10
Expiry: an exchanged token expires no later than the token it replaced.
.PP
J7 to J10 are whosent's consistency checks, derived from RFC 8693; no
specification defines them as a verification procedure.
.SS Biscuit
.TP
.B B1
Format: the token and every block decode, and each next key is valid.
.TP
.B B2
Version: the block's Datalog version is 3.0 to 3.3 and it uses only what that
version allows.
.TP
.B B3
Signature: each block's signature verifies. The authority block needs
\fB\-\-root\-key\fR.
.TP
.B B4
Third party: a third-party block's external signature verifies (v1).
.TP
.B B5
Proof: an open token's secret matches the last next key, or a sealed token's
seal verifies.
.TP
.B B6
Symbols: the block's symbols and keys do not overlap earlier ones, and every
reference resolves.
.TP
.B B7
Checks: the authorizer's facts and policies satisfy the token's checks.
.TP
.B B8
Revocation: no block's revocation id is on the \fB\-\-revoked\-jtis\fR list.
.SS UCAN
.TP
.B U1
Envelope: canonical DAG-CBOR with a varsig header and a well-formed
ucan/dlg@1.0.0 or ucan/inv@1.0.0 payload.
.TP
.B U2
Signature: the signature verifies against the issuer's did:key (Ed25519,
P-256, secp256k1).
.TP
.B U3
Time: nbf and exp hold at the evaluation time, within the skew.
.TP
.B U4
Principals: the first delegation is issued by its subject, and each token by
the audience of the delegation above it.
.TP
.B U5
Subject: every token is about the same subject; a powerline may not be the
root.
.TP
.B U6
Command: each command is the one above it or nested under it.
.TP
.B U7
Policy: the invocation's arguments satisfy every delegation's policy.
.TP
.B U8
Proofs: every proof the invocation names is supplied, or the invoker is the
subject.
.TP
.B U9
Revocation: no delegation's CID is on the \fB\-\-revoked\-jtis\fR list.
.SS Mandatum
.TP
.B V1
Structure: the link parses, is well formed, commits to its parent's digest, and
its depth follows the parent's.
.TP
.B V2
Custody: each link is issued by the subject of the link above it.
.TP
.B V3
Signature: the issuer signed these exact bytes, with a key you supplied.
.TP
.B V4
Time: not issued in the future and not expired, within the skew.
.TP
.B V5
Attenuation: authority only narrows. Capabilities, expiry, delegation depth,
sequence constraints and audience are no wider than the parent's.
.TP
.B V6
Sponsor: every link names the same human, authenticated the same way.
.TP
.B V7
Depth: the chain is no deeper than the sponsor allowed.
.TP
.B V8
Revocation: no link is revoked. Revoking a link revokes everything below it.
.TP
.B V9
Audience: the leaf is addressed to this resource server.
.SS Macaroon
.TP
.B M1
Format: the macaroons decode; a Fly.io attestation appears only in a proof.
.TP
.B M2
Signature: the HMAC chain matches under \fB\-\-root\-key\fR; a discharge's,
bound to its root.
.TP
.B M3
Discharge: every third-party caveat has its discharge, used once, and none is
left over.
.TP
.B M4
Binding: the caveat's sealed key opens under the chain; a Fly.io discharge is
bound to its token.
.TP
.B M5
Time: time caveats hold at the evaluation time.
.TP
.B M6
Caveat: first-party predicates are among the \fB\-\-fact\fR predicates.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B check
exits:
.TP
.B 0
VALID: every rule was checked for every link, and holds.
.TP
.B 1
INVALID: a rule failed, or could not be evaluated (an enforcement point denies).
.TP
.B 2
INCOMPLETE: nothing failed, but something was not checked. 1 with
\fB\-\-strict\fR.
.TP
.B 3
The command line or an input file is wrong.
.TP
.B 70
whosent and a format's reference implementation (the embedded Mandatum
verifier) disagree about the chain. This is a bug in whosent; please report it.
.PP
.B ask
exits 0 when the request is permitted by a VALID chain, 1 when it is not
(including any request against an INVALID chain), 2 when it is permitted
conditionally on rules that were not checked (1 with \fB\-\-strict\fR), and 3
and 70 as above. The exit status is unchanged by \fB\-\-json\fR.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
.TP
.B \(<-/h, \(->/l
Select a link (an arrow on the rail).
.TP
.B \(ua/k, \(da/j
Select a row of authority.
.TP
.BR J ", " K ", " pgup ", " pgdn
Scroll the link panel.
.TP
.B tab
Switch panel, when they do not fit side by side.
.TP
.B r
The raw link: a JWT's header and claims, a Biscuit block's Datalog and
signature, a Mandatum assertion's claims and digests. In a Mandatum chain a
parent digest takes the colour of the link it matches, so a spliced link is the
one whose colour matches nothing.
.TP
.B space
Replay authority flowing down the chain.
.TP
.B t
Time mode: a timeline of every link, and \(<- \(-> move the clock. The engine is
re-run at each step.
.TP
.BR [ ", " ] ", " shift+\(<- ", " shift+\(->
Move the clock in big steps.
.TP
.B 0
Back to the time the chain was opened at.
.TP
.B x
Simulate revoking the selected link; again to undo. Nothing is revoked.
Biscuit, UCAN and Mandatum only: JWTs have no standard revocation.
.TP
.B X
Clear every simulated revocation.
.TP
.B a
Ask whether the chain would permit a request.
.TP
.BR n ", " p
Next or previous scenario, in the demo tour.
.TP
.B ?
Show the full key binding help.
.TP
.BR q ", " esc
Quit, or go back.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to anything, draw without colour. Every state also has its own glyph,
so nothing is lost.
.TP
.B WHOSENT_ASCII
When set to 1, use ASCII glyphs only, as \fB\-\-ascii\fR does.
.SH SECURITY
Every string taken from a chain, a key document or a library error is escaped
before it reaches the terminal: control characters, escape sequences and bidi
overrides are shown as visible escapes, so a chain cannot rewrite the screen,
write the clipboard or disguise a name. \fB\-\-json\fR output is escaped the
same way.
.PP
whosent touches the network only to fetch key documents from the https URLs
you configure.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Take the tour:
.B whosent demo
.TP
Open a token from your IdP against its key document:
.B whosent token.jwt \-\-jwks https://idp.example.com=jwks.json \-\-audience https://api.example.com
.TP
Check a bundle of exchanged tokens the way a script would:
.B whosent demo \-\-emit fixture jwt\-bundle\-widened && cd fixture && whosent check tokens.json \-\-trust trust.json \-\-audience https://api.acme.example \-\-at 2026\-09\-12T08:01:00Z
.TP
Open a Biscuit token and run its checks for a read of orders:
.B whosent token.b64 \-\-root\-key root.pub \-\-fact 'resource("orders")' \-\-fact 'operation("read")'
.TP
Check a macaroon against its root key and the predicates a verifier satisfies:
.B whosent check token.txt \-\-root\-key root.key \-\-fact 'account = 3735928559'
.TP
Gate CI on a Mandatum chain that fully verifies:
.B whosent check chain.json \-\-trust trust.json \-\-revocations revocation.json \-\-audience https://mcp.example.org
.TP
Ask whether a Mandatum chain's leaf may fetch a page:
.B whosent ask chain.json \-\-resource\-id web.fetch \-\-tag external\-content \-\-attr args.host=docs.example.org
